isions and influence over the company  as a whole  so  it depends a little bit on the overall  culture and how exactly they're they're  treating remote employees i guess  but this is just a fair one to keep in  mind  number two networking is much harder for  remote workers  so this is actually  related to a separate point which is  their business travel right now in the  post pandemic era is way down  so if if business travel is at all  discretionary like conferences tend to  be  uh right now they're just being cut  because a lot of employers they just  don't want to deal with all of that and  all the risks and potential liabilities  associated with it  uh and then even when there are trips  they're getting scaled down considerably  and when that happens yeah that is going  to cut down on networking  so  this is this is just another one to uh  just another risk here to keep in mind  then you've got productivity  expectations may differ  um again this one is probably related to  the paradigm shift i mentioned before if  you were used to in the past you were  going into work and then you weren't  doing anything there but you felt like  you were productive just because you  were going in the office  well that might may not be the case  anymore  now this is a huge one  it can get lonely  now again this is this is one that's  going to be highly subjective  um there are people who are very  extroverted and they need to be out  there just in an office talking to  people all the time and i'm not i'm not  saying that like there's anything wrong  with that kind of framework in fact i  personally like being in a hybrid  environment because sometimes again i  like to cut down on the uh commute time  and all that kind of stuff as much as  anybody but i personally identify as a  pretty strong extrovert myself and so i  do like to interact and engage with  people so that's why the hybrid approach  is personally perfect for me  some people are really heavy on the on  the extrovert side  but there's also the  the the extreme on the other end which  is the heavy introverts  and now i have to extend a point of  caution on that one  because it is absolutely true there are  a lot of people out there who have in  historically anyway  derived a strong sense of community and  maybe family to an extent  uh from the from the office  uh dynamics that they experience  and now there's a and there's also a lot  of people that they are introverted and  if they don't have to go into an office  they're not going to have to even if  as far as their mental health and  overall well-being is concerned that  would overall be a good thing for  getting them out of their shell  so i have to just caution you on this  one if you're that type of person who  you  just benefit from going out and  interacting with people but you won't  you wouldn't unless you're forced to  working remotely could absolutely  destroy you in that sense so  you have to be really really dead honest  with yourself on that one if you're  falling on either of those two extremes  now if you're somebody who that doesn't  apply to you you have your sense of  community from your family or from your  church or from something else and you  really don't need  that kind of quality from your workplace  that's that's absolutely fine but if  previously you did  just get a really set a real sense of  community  from going in an office really be honest  with yourself about that  you've got self-directed learning  suddenly becomes crucial this one is  probably understandable because i'm  skeptical about is your boss going to  sit there and in on a longer more  extended microsoft teams or zoom call to  really hone in on your personal and  professional development i have my  doubts  uh remote work is a skill so  we'll have to explore this one a little  bit further in the future but i mean  that that is something that maybe you  could pipe yourself up on on on your  resume or in interviews maybe you did a  really really good job and you can  demonstrate that your productivity was  enhanced uh when you got the opportunity  to work remotely well if you can show  that then that's that's absolutely a net  positive so take advantage of that  and then as i was alluding to before  working remotely is not for everyone and  increasingly it looks like it's going to  be difficult to find a  a any kind of framework that's going to  work for everybody  and so if you were to ask me what i  think about the future of data science  work and is remote work in general a  good or a bad thing  i'd say the answer to that is there  really is no answer  i say that because number one the  conditions of the pandemic are changing  constantly nobody knows what that's  going to look like one year or five  years down the road  a lot of employers are a little confused  right now they're trying to react to  constantly changing conditions  they're trying to experiment with  different things to come up with an  ideal hybrid framework  and something that's going to support  their company culture as well as their  remote employees  but then employee preferences are very  different as you saw some do want to be  in an office all the time but  more people than not want to be remote  or hybrid so there's absolutely no one  solution out there that's going to  please everybody  and just this combination of factors i  think over the next three to five years  is going to contribute to a lot of just  uncertainty and just people  experimenting with different things and  the overall business climate changing  pretty rapidly  but for data scientists especially those  of you with a little bit more experience  i do think there's tons of reason for  encouragement here  so it's clear there's all this remote  work out there now that there's never  been in the past  and if you're somebody who's not  thrilled with your current employment  situation  whether you want to be in a remote  framework but your employer's forcing  you back in the office or honestly for  any other reason  there's tons of opportunities out there  that are going to allow you to to work  remotely  and even if you're somebody who wants to  stay in your current geographical  location like your state or your city  you have all this opportunity that could  be available to you that's that's in  different states maybe even in different  countries who knows  so there's all kinds of opportunity  available to you if you have skills that  are valuable in the marketplace which i  guarantee you that you do if you're a  proficient data scientist and you're  willing to put yourself out there  so i'll leave it at that  thanks for watching this video if you  enjoyed it please take a fraction of a  second to smash the like button  if you want to read any of these  articles that i went over in this video  they'll all be linked in the description  then i'll see you all in the not so  distant future  until then  richard on data
 

